# carts/digger — scripted walkthrough of the cave. Play it with:
#
#     quarp replay record carts/digger -o carts/digger/replays/walkthrough.qrpr \
#         --ticks 996 --input-file carts/digger/replays/walkthrough.input
#
# Grammar (quarp replay record --input-file): tick:buttons, entries separated by commas or
# newlines, '#' starts a comment. Buttons are L R U D O X S; an empty list releases
# everything, and what is named on a tick stays held until the next entry.
#
# The cave advances one grid step every 6 ticks. A press buys exactly one of those steps
# however long the key stays down; a key held past 18 ticks starts repeating, one cell every
# 6 ticks from then on (src/main.cs, LatchDirection). So a run of N cells is one entry, and
# it costs 18 ticks for the second cell and 6 for each one after it — the two grid steps in
# between are the gate, and the world keeps falling through them. Both facts are why this
# track is 996 ticks long and the pre-fix one was 768: same 128 cells, same route, 38 extra
# steps of standing still. A run must therefore not *start* under a rock it just loosened.
#
# What happens when — every line below was read back out of the cartridge itself, tick by
# tick, not just planned:
#
#   tick    6   the first press: one cell down, digging (1,2) — Mget(1,2) 1 -> 0
#   tick   12   the run east begins; ticks 18 and 24 are the gate, he stands, the world runs
#   tick   30   the hold repeats and keeps repeating, a cell every 6 ticks
#   tick   66   he digs (9,2) and the boulder at (9,1) loses its floor: Mget(9,1) 3 -> 8
#   tick   72   it drops into (9,2), one step behind the digger, who is already at (10,2)
#   tick   78   it lands in (9,2) and thuds: Mget(9,2) 8 -> 3
#   tick  102   he walks back into it and shoves it west into (8,2)
#   tick  186   gem 1 of 5, at (20,1)
#   tick  264   he digs (30,2); the boulder at (30,1) comes loose
#   tick  270   it drops into (30,2), one step behind him again
#   tick  276   it lands in (30,2) and thuds
#   tick  384   gem 2 of 5, at (16,4)
#   tick  444   gem 3 of 5, at (15,9)
#   tick  696   gem 4 of 5, at (5,15)
#   tick  924   gem 5 of 5, at (27,21) — quota met, the exit at (38,22) unlocks
#   tick  996   he steps into the open exit at (38,22) — LEVEL CLEAR
#
# 166 grid steps, 996 ticks, 128 of the steps move him; 5 of 7 gems, two left in the cave on
# purpose. The track is also the fix's own negative control: run it against the pre-fix latch
# (which re-armed from Btn every tick) and every hold overshoots — the digger misses the first
# gem, ends up at (18,19) with 2 of 5 and never opens the exit, let alone reaches it.
4:D,6:R,78:L,102:R,180:U,186:D,192:R,264:L,354:D,378:L,402:D,444:R,486:D,528:L,534:D,
564:L,636:U,660:L,696:R,732:D,756:R,834:D,870:R,990:D,996:
